{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786894775,"nonce":"Ee/9C4kY+xtmVLTgYSui1VNNrTc=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDORPa0Bs8AAAABM9vGxw","github-url":"https://github.com/FhG-IISB/jNO/issues/109","origin":"github"},"title":"r-adaptivity regressed in #97: relocation increases the energy error (adaptive_l_shape tutorial red on main)","message":"## What\n\n`docs/tutorial_examples/08_fem_and_varpinns/adaptive_l_shape.py` fails on `main`: r-adaptivity **increases** the energy-norm error instead of cutting it, so the tutorial's own assertion trips.\n\n```\nenergy-norm error  (E - E_ref),  E_ref = 0.36529\n  coarse start   : 4.459e-03   (92 dofs)\n  h-adaptivity   : 8.442e-04   (161 dofs, +69)   81% lower\n  r-adaptivity   : 4.982e-03   (92 dofs, +0)   -12% lower\n\nAssertionError: r-adaptivity should cut the energy error at fixed DOFs\n```\n\nDeterministic — identical to four digits across runs, and it takes 9.5 s, so it bisects cheaply. h-adaptivity in the same tutorial is unaffected (81% lower, well inside its gate).\n\n## When it broke\n\nBisected on `main` (2026-08-16, RTX 3070, float32, `pixi run -e fem`):\n\n| commit | r-adaptivity | |\n|---|---|---|\n| `9ecdfb6` — #94, where r-adaptivity landed | **55% lower** | passes |\n| `ca3ae14` — #97 | −12% | fails |\n| `c0aa3c3` #98 · `405c9c8` #105 · `96d1271` #106 | −12% | fails |\n\nSo it regressed in **#97** and has been red on `main` since. The tutorial's own diff across that PR is only the API rename `AdaptSpec(relocate=True, max_iters=60, lr=3e-3)` → `jno.solve.relocate(max_iters=60, lr=3e-3)`, same parameters — the change is behind the slot, not in what the tutorial asks for.\n\n## It is not a tuning drift\n\nEvery knob tried is still worse than not relocating at all:\n\n| setting | error | vs coarse |\n|---|---|---|\n| as the tutorial calls it | 4.982e-03 | −12% |\n| `quality_floor=0.0` | 4.982e-03 | −12% |\n| `relax=0` | 4.982e-03 | −12% |\n| `lr=3e-2` (10×) | 4.852e-03 | −9% |\n| `method=\"monge_ampere\"` | 4.645e-03 | −4% |\n\nMonge–Ampère is a separate algorithm from descent, so the fault is in something they **share** — the monitor, the trainable-vertex tagging, or how the relocated mesh is handed back — rather than in the descent step control.\n\nThe vertices do move, by `max |dx| = 0.078` on an `h = 0.12` mesh (over half an element), so relocation is confidently walking in a bad direction rather than doing nothing.\n\n## Where to look\n\nThe shared path between the two methods, entered from `jno.solve.relocate` → the adapt slot in `fem.solve`. #97 is a large PR (\"1D parity, adaptivity, block-assembly unification, eigensolver and fail-loud fixes\"); the adaptivity part of it is the place to start, and `9ecdfb6` is a known-good reference to diff the relocation path against.\n\nOne loose end worth redoing properly rather than trusting: re-solving from scratch on the relocated mesh gave `2.7e-02`, six times worse than the coarse start — but that was produced by mutating `mesh.points` by hand and calling `_apply_mesh`, which may not rebuild everything the normal path does. If it holds up under a clean rebuild it says the relocated **mesh** is genuinely bad (monitor/direction), not that the returned solution is stale against it.\n\n## Also worth a regression test\n\nThe measurement the tutorial makes — relocation must cut the error at fixed DOFs — exists only in the tutorial, which is why this reached `main` and sat through three releases. A test asserting it on this L-shape would have caught #97 at review.","files":null}]}